              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="tfa-seq-rail"><span class="tfa-seq-dot"></span></div>
            <blockquote class="tfa-seq-quote">"He did not sleep at night. He tried not to think about Ikemefuna, but the more he tried the more he thought about him. Once he got up from bed and walked about his compound. But he was so weak that his legs could hardly carry him. He felt like a drunken giant walking with the limbs of a mosquito."</blockquote>
          </article>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <div class="tfa-passage">
        <div class="tfa-passage-body">
          <p>The killing of Ikemefuna is the hinge of this strand of the novel's meaning. The Oracle decrees Ikemefuna must die. Obierika tells Okonkwo he has no obligation to participate — but he participates anyway, "because he was afraid of being thought weak." He strikes the killing blow on a boy who called him "father" because his definition of strength has no space for the act of stepping aside.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-passage">
        <div class="tfa-passage-body">
          <p>In the night that follows — unable to sleep, thinking of Ezinma as the daughter who "should have been a boy," yet unable to grieve for Ikemefuna — this is the novel's earliest and most precise measurement of what the masculine ideal extracts from the men who embody it. His tragedy is simultaneously personal and cultural: the novel suggests that the same values that made Igbo civilization great also produced a structure with built-in vulnerabilities that colonialism could exploit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

